---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Toast Doctrine"
  description="A Spanning Tree memory system where BPDU = toast, TCN = burnt toast, and the root bridge is the toaster."
>
  <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">null route queen / ccnp journey / spanning tree</p>

      <h1 id="article-title" class="page-title">Toast Doctrine</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        Spanning Tree finally started sticking when I stopped treating it like
        protocol soup and started treating the network like a kitchen appliance.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="why-toast">
      <h2 id="why-toast">Why toast?</h2>

      <p>
        I needed a memory trick that made STP feel less abstract.
        So the doctrine became:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>BPDU = toast</strong></li>
        <li><strong>TCN = burnt toast</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Root bridge = the toaster</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Is it technically ridiculous? Yes.
        Does it work? Also yes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="root">
      <h2 id="root">The toaster: root bridge</h2>

      <p>
        STP begins by deciding which switch becomes the root bridge.
        Once the root is elected, every other switch works out its best path toward it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In Toast Doctrine terms, the root bridge is the toaster everyone agrees
        is the center of the breakfast universe.
      </p>

      <p>
        Lower bridge ID wins. Priority matters first, then MAC address breaks the tie.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="bpdu">
      <h2 id="bpdu">BPDU = toast</h2>

      <p>
        BPDUs are the control messages switches use to talk about the STP topology.
        They carry the information needed to elect the root and maintain the tree.
      </p>

      <p>
        If toast is moving around the network, the switches are still talking.
        If the expected toast disappears, something changed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="tcn">
      <h2 id="tcn">TCN = burnt toast</h2>

      <p>
        A Topology Change Notification means something in the Layer 2 topology changed
        and the rest of the network needs to react.
      </p>

      <p>
        Burnt toast is the smell that gets everyone's attention.
        Something changed, and now the network has to update what it knows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="convergence">
      <h2 id="convergence">Why old STP convergence feels painfully slow</h2>

      <p>
        Traditional 802.1D STP can take up to roughly 50 seconds to reconverge
        in a classic failure scenario: 20 seconds of Max Age, then 15 seconds
        Listening and 15 seconds Learning.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is an eternity when somebody is staring at a dead link and asking
        whether the network is broken.
      </p>

      <p>
        The lesson for me was not just memorizing the timer.
        It was understanding why newer STP variants try to converge faster.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="guards">
      <h2 id="guards">Guardrails around the toaster</h2>

      <p>
        The guard features started making more sense once I stopped memorizing
        names and focused on what kind of bad behavior each one prevents.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>BPDU Guard</strong> — protects edge ports from unexpected BPDUs.</li>
        <li><strong>Root Guard</strong> — prevents an unexpected switch from becoming root.</li>
        <li><strong>Loop Guard</strong> — helps protect against loops when expected BPDUs disappear.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Different guard, different failure mode.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 id="lesson">The real lesson</h2>

      <p>
        STP is not magic. It is a deterministic conversation between switches
        about who is root, which paths are preferred, and which links need to stay blocked.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once I could picture the conversation, the acronyms stopped feeling random.
      </p>

      <p>
        And if all else fails:
        <strong>BPDU = toast. TCN = burnt toast. Root bridge = toaster.</strong>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <p><a href="/ccnp-journey/">← Back to CCNP Journey</a></p>
</SiteLayout>
